//// `Array.reverse()` answers a NEW array and leaves the receiver alone —
//// front 00 · 04-js round 2, D5.
////
//// Native `Array.prototype.reverse` reverses in place and answers the same
//// array, so commonJS lowered `val rev = xs.reverse();` to something that
//// also reversed `xs`, where `lists:reverse/1` on erlang and the wasm
//// lowering both leave it untouched. Any fold that reads the receiver again
//// after reversing it therefore answered one thing on commonJS and another
//// on the other two targets, at exit 0 — a library maps its list twice
//// rather than call `reverse()` for its return value.
////
//// The cell reads the receiver AFTER the call, which is the only way to see
//// it: the reversed answer was right on every target the whole time.

pub fn main() {
    val xs = [1, 2, 3];
    val rev = xs.reverse();
    @print(rev.join(","));
    @print(xs.join(","));
    @print(xs.reverse().join(","));
    @print(xs.join(","));
}
